/*
# Tree

An outline whose rows open and close to show their children — a file browser, the
sidebar of a mail program or a Finder window. Like `Segmented`'s choices and `Menu`'s
items, what it shows is **data**: nested records, each `{ id, label, icon?, children? }`.

```declare-fragment
Tree [ width = 240, accessibilityLabel = "Files",
    items = { app.folders },
    value = { app.picked }, input(v: string) { app.picked = v },
    expanded = { app.openFolders }, expandInput(ids: array) { app.openFolders = ids }
    ]
```

The Tree holds two values, and both follow the value pattern: **`value`**, the id of the
selected row, and **`expanded`**, the ids of the rows that are open. Neither is written
into the records — the records say what the outline holds, the two values say where the
person is in it — so the same records can stand under two trees open in different
places. Each value works standalone, or is owned by the program through `input` and
`expandInput`.

The rows on screen are the open part of the outline, flattened into a dataset and
replicated as `TreeRow`s. Every row is built, none virtualized: a tree is for tens or
hundreds of rows, not hundreds of thousands. For a long flat collection use a `Table`.

### Keyboard

The Tree is **one Tab stop**: Tab lands on the selected row (else the first) and the
next Tab leaves. Focus itself moves from row to row, so a screen reader announces each:

- **Up** and **Down** move to the previous and next visible row; **Home** and **End** to
  the first and last.
- **Right** opens a closed row; on an open one it moves to its first child.
- **Left** closes an open row; on any other it moves to the row's parent.
- **Enter** and **Space** select the row focus is on.

With the pointer, a click selects a row, a click on its chevron opens or closes it
without selecting, and a second click on a row with children opens or closes it.

### Naming it

A tree is something a person moves through, so a screen reader needs its name: set
`accessibilityLabel` where you place it ("Files", "Mailboxes"). The rows are named by
their labels.

```declare
App [ width = 320, height = 300, picked: string = "notes",
    Tree [ x = 20, y = 20, width = 280, accessibilityLabel = "Documents",
        expanded = ["docs"],
        value = { app.picked }, input(v: string) { app.picked = v },
        items = { [
            ({ id: "docs", label: "Documents", children: [
                ({ id: "notes", label: "Notes.txt" }),
                ({ id: "plans", label: "Plans", children: [
                    ({ id: "q3", label: "Third quarter" }),
                    ({ id: "q4", label: "Fourth quarter" }) ] }) ] }),
            ({ id: "pics", label: "Pictures", children: [
                ({ id: "beach", label: "Beach.jpg" }) ] }),
            ({ id: "todo", label: "To do" }) ] }
        ],
    Text [ x = 20, y = 260, text = { "selected: " + app.picked } ]
    ]
```

## items
The outline, as **records**: `{ id, label, icon?, children? }`, where `children` is an
array of the same records, nested to any depth. `id` is a string and unique across the
whole tree — selection and the open rows are held by it. `icon` names an icon class, as
`Menu`'s items do (`icon: "CheckIcon"`); a name computed at run time needs
`use [ Name ]` to survive the build. A row with a `children` array is a parent even when
the array is empty.

## value
The **id of the selected row**, `""` for none. Constrain it to your own state and
override `input` to own it, or leave it alone and read it back. Selecting a row inside a
closed parent leaves the parent closed: open the ancestors through `expanded` to show it.

## input()
**Where the selection goes**, receiving the picked id. The default writes `value`;
override it to own the destination.

## select()
Selects a row by id, routing through `input`. What a click, Enter and Space call.

## expanded
The **ids of the open rows**. A row whose parent is closed stays hidden whatever this
says, and opens again as it was when its parent does. Constrain it and override
`expandInput` to own it, exactly as `value` with `input`.

## expandInput()
**Where the open rows go**, receiving the whole new list of ids. The default writes
`expanded`; override it to own the destination.

## isOpen()
Whether a row is open, by id.

## openRow()
Opens a row by id, routing through `expandInput`. Does nothing to a row already open.

## closeRow()
Closes a row by id, routing through `expandInput`. Its descendants keep their own
open state for when it opens again.

## toggle()
Opens a closed row, closes an open one. What a click on the chevron and a second click on
a row call.

## disabled
Disables the whole tree: it dims, its rows take no pointer or key, and it leaves the Tab
order.

## opacity
Disabled dims the whole tree to the theme's `disabledOpacity`.

## indent
How far each level sits inside its parent, 16 by default.

## hasIcons
Whether any record, at any depth, carries an `icon`. Decides whether there is an icon
column at all, so that every label in the tree starts on the same line whether or not
its own row has an icon.

## anyIcon()
Whether any record in a list, at any depth, carries an `icon`.

## flatten()
The visible rows, in order: the outline walked from the top, descending only into open
rows. Each row record wraps its source record (`item`) and adds what the row shows from
the outline — `level` (1 at the top), `hasChildren`, `open`, the `parent` row's id, and
its place among its siblings, `pos` of `size`. The tree's own dataset of these rows,
re-derived whenever `items` or `expanded` changes, is what the `TreeRow`s replicate over;
the rows stack flush.

## rowAt()
The row view at a visible index, or null. A handler-only walk — a constraint may not
aggregate the view tree.

## rowFor()
The row view showing an id, or null when that row is not visible. A handler-only walk.

## focusRow()
Moves focus to a row, and scrolls it into view when the tree scrolls. The keyboard's
moves land here.

## _clickId
**Internal.** The row last clicked, which the next click pairs with.

## _clickAt
**Internal.** When it was clicked, in milliseconds.

## focusScope
`stop`: the tree is one Tab stop, and focus moves among its rows with the keys above.

## focusEntry()
The row Tab lands on: the selected one when it is visible, else the first.

## accessibilityRole
`tree`.

## accessibilityLabel
The tree's name, `""` by default: **the use site names it**, since only the program
knows what the tree holds.

## width
240 by default. The rows are as wide as the tree, so the tree does not size itself to
its labels; a label too long for its row ends in an ellipsis.

## datapath
Roots the tree on its own `shown` data, so the rows replicate without the author writing a datapath.
*/

include [ "control.declare", "icons/core.declare", "icons/icon.declare" ]

class Tree [
    // an outline a person moves through: a tree to a screen reader, its rows
    // tree items; the use site names it

    accessibilityRole = tree,
    accessibilityLabel = "",

    items: array = [],
    disabled: boolean = false,
    width = 240,
    indent: number = 16,
    opacity = { disabled ? (provided("theme").disabledOpacity) : 1 },

    // ── the two values, each the value pattern ────────────────────────────

    value: string = "",
    input(v: string) { value = v },
    select(id: string) { input(id) },

    expanded: array = [],
    expandInput(ids: array) { expanded = ids },
    isOpen(id: string) -> boolean { return (this.expanded ?? []).includes(id) },
    openRow(id: string) { if (!this.isOpen(id)) expandInput((this.expanded ?? []).concat([id])) },
    closeRow(id: string) { if (this.isOpen(id)) expandInput((this.expanded ?? []).filter((x) => x !== id)) },
    toggle(id: string) { if (this.isOpen(id)) closeRow(id); else openRow(id) },

    // ── the rows on screen: the open part of the outline, flattened ───────
    //
    // Each row record wraps its source record rather than copying it, and
    // carries its id at the top so the reconciler keeps one row per id —
    // which is what lets a chevron turn on its own row instead of a new one.

    flatten(list: array, open: array) -> array {
        const out = []
        const walk = (rs: any[], level: number, parentId: string) => {
            const n = (rs ?? []).length
            for (let i = 0; i < n; i++) {
                const it = rs[i]
                const id = "" + it.id
                const kids = Array.isArray(it.children)
                const isOpen = kids && open.includes(id)
                out.push({ id: id, item: it, label: "" + (it.label ?? ""), icon: it.icon ?? null,
                    level: level, hasChildren: kids, open: isOpen, parent: parentId, pos: i + 1, size: n })
                if (isOpen) walk(it.children, level + 1, id)
                }
            }
        walk(list, 1, "")
        return out
        },

    anyIcon(list: array) -> boolean {
        return (list ?? []).some((it) => it.icon != null || (Array.isArray(it.children) && this.anyIcon(it.children)))
        },
    hasIcons: boolean = { this.anyIcon(this.items ?? []) },

    shown: Dataset [ contents = { { rows: classroot.flatten(classroot.items ?? [], classroot.expanded ?? []) } } ],

    // the row walks — handlers only; a constraint may not aggregate the tree

    rowAt(i: number) -> View? {
        return (this.children.find((c) => (c as TreeRow)._isTreeRow === true && c.rowIndex == i) ?? null) as View | null
        },
    rowFor(id: string) -> View? {
        return (this.children.find((c) => (c as TreeRow)._isTreeRow === true && (c as TreeRow).rowId == id) ?? null) as View | null
        },
    focusRow(v: View?) {
        if (v == null) return
        Focus.focus(v)
        if (Focus.getFocus() == v) v.scrollIntoView("nearest")
        },

    // the second click a row pairs with. Not the platform's onDblClick: that
    // makes every single click wait out the double window, and a click on a
    // row must select at once.

    _clickId: string = "",
    _clickAt: number = 0,

    // one Tab stop, landing on the selected row; focus moves among the rows

    focusScope = stop,
    focusEntry() -> View? { return this.rowFor(this.value) ?? this.rowAt(0) },

    layout: SimpleLayout [ axis = y, spacing = 0 ],
    datapath = { this.shown.value },
    TreeRow [ datapath = :rows[] ]
    ]


/*
# TreeRow

One row of a `Tree`, **materialized from the tree's visible rows — never authored
directly**. It is a `Control`, so focus lands on the row itself and a screen reader
announces it as a tree item, with its level, whether it is open, and whether it is
selected.

A row is the library's row look: a rounded highlight, `controlHovered` under the pointer
and `controlSelected` when selected, the label in the theme's `text` at the menu's size.
A row with children leads with a chevron that turns on a spring as it opens and closes,
the same turn as an `Accordion` pane's; a row without one leaves the space blank, so
labels at one level line up.

```declare
App [ width = 280, height = 140,
    Tree [ x = 20, y = 20, accessibilityLabel = "Mailboxes", expanded = ["in"],
        items = { [ ({ id: "in", label: "Inbox", children: [
                        ({ id: "work", label: "Work" }), ({ id: "home", label: "Home" }) ] }),
                    ({ id: "sent", label: "Sent" }) ] }
        ]
    ]
```

## _isTreeRow
**Internal.** The marker the tree's walks key on — not API.

## rowId
This row's id, from its record.

## level
How deep the row sits, 1 at the top.

## hasChildren
Whether the row's record has `children` — whether it opens at all.

## open
Whether the row is open, from the tree's `expanded`.

## selected
Whether this row is the tree's `value`.

## twist
The chevron's angle, in radians, sprung between 0 (open, pointing down) and −π/2
(closed, pointing right), so it turns as the row opens rather than swapping marks. It
starts where it belongs, so a row that appears does not turn into place.

## inset
Where the row's content begins: its level's indent, plus a small margin.

## ink
The label's colour: the theme's `text`, which stays readable on the selected tint in
every theme.

## press()
Selects this row. What Enter and Space call.

## onClick
Selects this row; a second click on a row with children within the double-click
interval opens or closes it.

## onKeyDown
The tree's keys — Up, Down, Home, End, Right, Left, Enter and Space — as `Tree`
describes them. Other keys go to `Control`.

## focusShape()
Rings the row's rounded highlight, not its whole box.

## disabled
Disabled with the tree; a row never disables alone.

## width
As wide as the tree.

## height
The theme's `menuRowHeight`.

## accessibilityRole
`treeItem`.

## accessibilityLevel
The row's `level`.

## accessibilityPosInSet
The row's place among its siblings, from 1 — not its place in the flattened list.

## accessibilitySetSize
How many siblings the row is among.

## accessibilityExpanded
`true` or `false` for a row with children; unset for a leaf, which opens nothing.

## accessibilitySelected
Follows `selected`.
*/

class TreeRow extends Control [
    _isTreeRow: boolean = true,

    width = 100%,
    height = { provided("theme").menuRowHeight },
    disabled = { (parent as Tree).disabled },

    rowId: string = { "" + (:id ?? "") },
    level: number = { Number(:level ?? 1) },
    hasChildren: boolean = { :hasChildren == true },
    open: boolean = { :open == true },
    selected: boolean = { (parent as Tree).value === this.rowId && this.rowId != "" },

    accessibilityRole = treeItem,
    accessibilityLevel = { level },
    accessibilityPosInSet = { :pos },
    accessibilitySetSize = { :size },
    accessibilityExpanded = { hasChildren ? open : null },
    accessibilitySelected = { selected },

    inset: number = { 6 + (this.level - 1) * (parent as Tree).indent },

    press() { if (!disabled) (parent as Tree).select(rowId) },

    onClick() {
        if (disabled) return
        const t = parent as Tree
        const now = Date.now()
        const second = t._clickId == rowId && now - t._clickAt < 400
        t._clickId = second ? "" : rowId
        t._clickAt = now
        if (second && hasChildren) t.toggle(rowId)
        else press()
        },

    onKeyDown(e: KeyEvent) {
        if (disabled) return
        const t = parent as Tree
        if (e.ctrl || e.alt || e.meta) { super.onKeyDown(e); return }
        const n = (t.shown.value?.rows ?? []).length
        if (e.key == "ArrowDown") t.focusRow(t.rowAt(Math.min(n - 1, rowIndex + 1)))
        else if (e.key == "ArrowUp") t.focusRow(t.rowAt(Math.max(0, rowIndex - 1)))
        else if (e.key == "Home") t.focusRow(t.rowAt(0))
        else if (e.key == "End") t.focusRow(t.rowAt(n - 1))
        else if (e.key == "ArrowRight") {
            if (hasChildren && !open) t.openRow(rowId)
            else if (hasChildren) {
                // an open row whose `children` is empty has no first child to move to
                const c = t.rowAt(rowIndex + 1)
                if (c != null && (c as TreeRow).level > level) t.focusRow(c)
                }
            }
        else if (e.key == "ArrowLeft") {
            if (hasChildren && open) t.closeRow(rowId)
            else t.focusRow(t.rowFor("" + (:parent ?? "")))
            }
        else if ((e.key == "Enter" || e.key == " ") && !e.repeat) press()
        else super.onKeyDown(e)
        },

    // the ring hugs the rounded highlight

    focusShape() { return { x: 0, y: 1, w: this.width, h: this.height - 2, rad: provided("theme").controlCornerRadius } },

    // the highlight, inset a point top and bottom so a hovered row beside the
    // selected one reads as two

    hl: View [ y = 1, width = 100%, height = { parent.height - 2 },
        cornerRadius = { provided("theme").controlCornerRadius },
        stateFill: Color? = { classroot.pressed ? provided("theme").controlPressed : classroot.hovered ? provided("theme").controlHovered : null },
        fill = { classroot.selected ? provided("theme").controlSelected : this.stateFill }
        ],

    // The chevron turns rather than swapping between two marks, on a spring,
    // as an Accordion pane's does. It is its own hit target: a click on it opens
    // or closes the row and leaves the selection where it was.

    twist: number = { :open == true ? 0 : -Math.PI / 2 },
    Spring [ attribute = twist, to = { classroot.open ? 0 : -Math.PI / 2 },
        stiffness = 220, damping = 26, epsilon = 0.004 ],

    disc: View [ x = { classroot.inset - 3 }, width = 20, height = 100%,
        visible = { classroot.hasChildren },
        onClick() { if (!classroot.disabled) (classroot.parent as Tree).toggle(classroot.rowId) },
        chev: ChevronIcon [ x = 3, y = center, iconSize = 14,
            angle = { classroot.twist }, ink = { provided("theme").textMuted } ]
        ],

    ink: Color = { provided("theme").text },

    ico: IconHost [ y = center, x = { classroot.inset + 18 }, iconSize = 16, kind = { "" + (:icon ?? "") }, ink = { classroot.ink } ],

    lbl: TextLabel [ wrap = false, maxLines = 1,
        x = { classroot.inset + 18 + ((classroot.parent as Tree).hasIcons ? 22 : 0) },
        width = { Math.max(0, parent.width - this.x - 8) },
        fontSize = { provided("theme").menuFontSize },
        textColor = { classroot.ink },
        text = { "" + (:label ?? "") } ]
    ]
